            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Michael R. Fontham (Respondent), according to Michael R. Fontham (Respondent), the case concerns a contract amendment approved by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) that set conditions for a utility to bill an affiliate for mothballed generating units."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Michael R. Fontham (Respondent)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "eca285aa5e1427cb3065c410a46686549d519a1c4e8f185f8a719010a1f9f25a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-eca285aa5e14-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 27,
              "start": 27
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Michael R. Fontham (Respondent), according to Michael R. Fontham (Respondent), the utility failed to follow those procedures, did not consider the conditions, and did not record the decision in its minutes, as required."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Fontham",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "eca285aa5e1427cb3065c410a46686549d519a1c4e8f185f8a719010a1f9f25a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-eca285aa5e14-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 40,
              "start": 40
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Fontham, the parties dispute whether the Federal Power Act preempts the state from enforcing the contract terms, with the respondent arguing it does not."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae) via Austin C. Schlick",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "eca285aa5e1427cb3065c410a46686549d519a1c4e8f185f8a719010a1f9f25a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-eca285aa5e14-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 18,
              "start": 18
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to United States (Amicus Curiae) via Austin C. Schlick, the amicus brief argues that regulation of wholesale rates for electric energy in interstate commerce is exclusively within FERC\u2019s jurisdiction."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "eca285aa5e1427cb3065c410a46686549d519a1c4e8f185f8a719010a1f9f25a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-eca285aa5e14-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Court noted a general prohibition on \u201ctrapping\u201d and referenced a FERC\u2011approved rate schedule that determines how the allocation should be carried out."
      }
    ],
